#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:5
#: dcd88b7a95d5413486c7b6fe8ce5f2f7
msgid "ACRO Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:7
#: 1c91ce98ba6c407cbc7679576fb22eca
msgid "ACRO (for acrobatic) is a mode for advanced users that provides rate based stabilization with optional attitude lock. It is a good choice for people who want to push their plane harder than you can in :ref:`FLY BY WIRE A (FBWA) <fbwa-mode>` or :ref:`STABILIZE <stabilize-mode>` mode without flying in :ref:`MANUAL <manual-mode>`. This is the mode to use for rolls, loops and other basic aerobatic maneuvers, or if you just want an \"on rails\" manual flying mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:14
#: c1d730760e0f48ebbe9cb04dd21d25ff
msgid "rate stabilization is not enabled by default for the YAW axis. Set :ref:`YAW_RATE_ENABLE<YAW_RATE_ENABLE>` = 1 to enable yaw axis rate stabilization. Be careful since this will prevent any turns by aileron alone and require application of rudder also to turn. Also, do not enable this if the plane has no yaw control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:16
#: 9ac96bfb22604aa2a914f9b7192c7e7b
msgid "To use this mode you need to set up :ref:`ACRO_YAW_RATE<ACRO_YAW_RATE>` (if using yaw rate controller), :ref:`ACRO_ROLL_RATE <ACRO_ROLL_RATE>` and :ref:`ACRO_PITCH_RATE <ACRO_PITCH_RATE>`. These default to 180 degrees/second (and 0, ie no limit, for yaw.However, for AUTOTUNE on yaw axis to work, it must be set to a non-zero value. 90 degrees/second is suggested), and control how responsive your plane will be about each axis. It is also necessary to have the plane tuned well (see :ref:`tuning-quickstart`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:20
#: 96aaadd673f24f18abfdc71685f82895
msgid "When flying in ACRO the aircraft will resist changes to its existing attitude if you have no stick input. So if you roll the plane to a 30 degree bank angle with 10 degrees pitch and then let go of the sticks, the plane should hold that attitude short term. This applies upside down as well, so if you roll the plane upside down and let go of the sticks the plane will try to hold the inverted attitude short term or until you move the sticks again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:27
#: 792888c1e56548c3a47ebd6d4919569e
msgid "the internal controllers will resist attitude changes, but drift due to turbulence or miss-trimming will result in gradual attitude changes. See ACRO MODE ATTITUDE LOCKING section below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:29
#: 3cf1e0f619b84e9ca36f9d96c36f8fa4
msgid "When you apply aileron or elevator stick the plane will rotate about that axis (in body frame) at a rate proportional to the amount of stick movement. So if you apply half deflection on the aileron stick then the plane will start rolling at half of ``ACRO_ROLL_RATE``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:34
#: c6386c6e71a24e798e0c14dbe4580422
msgid "the :ref:`MAN_EXPO_ROLL<MAN_EXPO_ROLL>`, :ref:`MAN_EXPO_PITCH<MAN_EXPO_PITCH>`, and :ref:`MAN_EXPO_RUDDER<MAN_EXPO_RUDDER>` parameters will apply exponential to the stick inputs, if non-zero, in this mode. This is for users with transmitters which do not provide this function and desire to \"soften\" stick feel around neutral."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:36
#: 69356fc766374bc49e79cd7dcaf8b1a0
msgid "So to perform a simple horizontal roll, just start in level flight then hold the aileron stick hard over while leaving the elevator stick alone. The plane will apply elevator correction to try to resist pitch changes while rolling, including applying inverse elevator while inverted. But to exactly hold the pitch attitude during multiple rolls without drift, :ref:`ACRO_LOCKING<ACRO_LOCKING>` must be enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:42
#: 58e75d3eafd945a3aa4e13436b644cbe
msgid "Performing a loop is just as simple - just start with wings level then pull back on the elevator stick while leaving the aileron alone. The controller will try to hold your roll attitude through the loop. You can stop the loop upside down if you like as part of maneuvers such as Immelman turns or cuban eights."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:48
#: ce50f53f1fba403ea15054a2cb654f4a
msgid "Note that if you are using ACRO mode to try and teach yourself aerobatic flying then it is highly recommended that you setup a :ref:`geo-fence <geofencing>` in case you get disoriented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:54
#: aad7cbf3982a4ef4b44979b5eeb83a68
msgid "It is very easy to stall your plane in ACRO mode, and if you stall you should change to MANUAL mode to recover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:57
#: 964fbc09ed4045fd8795709505c54645
msgid "make sure you know the limitations of your airframe, and what the correct stall recovery procedure is. This varies a lot between airframes. Search for stall recovery tutorials for R/C aircraft and read them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:61
#: 16f2bf46a3584b75a4996a2c518f2916
msgid "don't overload your airframe, only fly ACRO mode with a plane capable of surviving full control surface deflections at any speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:62
#: 4d550ddbe3ea43eaa84dac8e94eb86c2
msgid "make sure you have enough airspeed for whatever maneuver you are attempting. Throttle and speed control is completely under manual pilot control in ACRO mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:65
#: 16d82a2d89bf4fac8b30b33a0cb4cb50
msgid "practice stall recovery before trying anything too fancy. Make sure you practice when you have plenty of altitude to give you time to try different recovery strategies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:69
#: 6ce64d05e69a45d48d6c08527378866e
msgid "It can be a lot of fun flying ACRO mode, but you can also easily stall and crash hard. Automatic stall detection and recovery in autopilots is an area of research, and is not yet implemented in Plane, so if you do stall then recovery is up to you. The best mode for recovery is MANUAL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:75
#: 07f589bb261843b38ae69c25b3843151
msgid "ACRO MODE ATTITUDE LOCKING"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:77
#: 0b0ab8515c9747189fe83f3b7a0b356a
msgid "By enabling the :ref:`ACRO_LOCKING<ACRO_LOCKING>` parameter, whatever attitude (roll and pitch angle) the pilot places the plane in, upon releasing the sticks, the autopilot will not only resist rate changes (caused by trim or turbulence), but also attempt to hold and correct back to that attitude. Note that his requires that the plane be properly tuned (see :ref:`Tuning<common-tuning>` )."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:79
#: 5dbe7edbf4b64d05a2538b063833784b
msgid "It is recommended that it be set to \"2\", instead of \"1\", in order to use a quarternion based control system with much better performance than the older system. In order for this to be effective, yaw rate control (:ref:`YAW_RATE_ENABLE<YAW_RATE_ENABLE>`) must be \"1\" and the yaw rate controller tuned using :ref:`Autotune <automatic-tuning-with-autotune>` for best performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:82
#: edc151b17f934991a44e39e865eda133
msgid "ACRO Mode YAW Rate Control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:84
#: c5c5ae2ac49443ea947d442c56164ffc
msgid "As of version 4.2, ArduPilot provides the option for utilization of a rate controller for YAW, which behaves in the same manner as the pitch and roll controllers, explained above, but for the YAW axis controlled by the Rudder stick, assuming the vehicle has a rudder control surface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:86
#: fd460eb9822d48a5a14730d31a5f5d4d
msgid "To enable this functionality, set :ref:`YAW_RATE_ENABLE<YAW_RATE_ENABLE>` to 1. When enabled, the :ref:`ACRO_YAW_RATE<ACRO_YAW_RATE>` parameter can be used to adjust maximum yaw rate demanded at rudder stick full deflections in ACRO mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:88
#: 33ce79682f58455096b8aed15fd4e13c
msgid "Before use, the controller should be tuned, either manually or using AutoTune. See :ref:`automatic-tuning-with-autotune` or the YAW tuning section of the :ref:`Manual Tuning page<new-roll-and-pitch-tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/acro-mode.rst:90
#: 53c23f164b90433eba8923d1512608c7
msgid "using this controller will give the feel of a 'heading hold' yaw axis. While not exactly \"heading\" holding, it does resist any yaw rate change not commanded by the pilot. This means the pilot will need to \"fly the tail\" in turns. Just banking will not generate a clean turn."
msgstr ""
